diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
index ae225b3..f640b76 100644
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -43,6 +43,20 @@ Change the limits in `~/.pi/agent/infinite-context/settings.json` and restart Pi
 
 `nudgeAtTokens` can also be a range like `[100000, 300000]`. Each session then draws its own limit, which is how the experiment above works. Sessions record their limits and reminders, so tools like [context-efficiency](https://github.com/fdietze/context-efficiency) can analyze them.
 
+`overrides` sets limits per provider or per exact `provider/model`. Each field comes from the current model's own rule, then its provider's, then the top level:
+
+```json
+{
+  "nudgeAtTokens": 150000,
+  "overrides": {
+    "anthropic": { "nudgeAtTokens": [100000, 300000] },
+    "anthropic/claude-opus-5-5": { "nudgeAtTokens": 400000, "nudgeAtPercent": 50 }
+  }
+}
+```
+
+A rule's range is drawn like the top level's, and the record names the rule that set the limit (`rule`). A switch to a model whose rule differs records the new settings; a different range draws a new limit.
+
 Pi's own compaction, including `/compact`, is blocked. If the agent does not fold enough, the context can still overflow.
 
 ## When a fold pays off
